/-! # Étale source-and-target locality of scheme lci morphisms

Source: Stacks Project, spaces-more-morphisms.tex, preamble to Tag 06C4;
more-morphisms.tex, Tags 069F, 069N and 069P; descent.tex, Tag 04R2
(blueprint: lem-sfb-scheme-lci-etale-locality).

The scheme lci predicate is reused from RelativeFiniteHilbertGroupoids. It means
locally a closed immersion with finite Koszul-regular ideal generators followed
by a smooth map. No finiteness, separation, Noetherianity or flatness of the
original morphism is assumed.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- The Koszul lci property respects isomorphisms and satisfies all three clauses
of étale source-and-target locality, including pointwise detection by compatible
étale charts. Source: Stacks Project, preamble to Tag 06C4, Tags 069F, 069N,
069P and 04R2 (blueprint: lem-sfb-scheme-lci-etale-locality).

Proof:

1. Work with the property $P$ as stated. Its witnesses restrict to source opens: restrict the
closed immersion to an open of its source and then restrict the ambient scheme to an open on
which it is closed; the smooth map restricts by [lem-pc-open-immersion-smooth-composition], and
near each point of the restricted closed subscheme the ideal is still generated by a finite
Koszul-regular sequence on a smaller affine open by [new-s3-koszul-regular-affine-refinement]
(points of the ambient open outside the closed subscheme have unit ideal, generated by the
Koszul-regular sequence $1$). Such witnesses therefore glue as a property on an open cover of
the source. For an open $V\subseteq Y$, restrict a witness through $Q\to Y$ to $Q\times_YV$, an
open of $Q$; conversely compose its smooth map to $V$ with $V\hookrightarrow Y$, which is etale
by [lem-weil-pbc-open-immersion-etale], hence smooth by
[lem-weil-pbc-etale-smooth-zero-dimensional-fibres]; the composite is smooth by
[lem-trf-smooth-composition]. Thus $P(f|_{f^{-1}V}:f^{-1}V\to V)$ is equivalent to $P$ on that
source open with target $Y$. Isomorphisms carry the same affine ideals and Koszul complexes to
isomorphic ones, so they preserve the witnesses. These observations also show that an immersion
which is locally Koszul-regular followed by a smooth map gives $P$: locally replace the
immersion by a closed immersion into an ambient open.

2. Every morphism with $P$ is locally of finite type. Indeed, locally its ambient smooth map is
given by a finitely presented algebra, hence by finitely many algebra generators; taking a
quotient for the closed immersion leaves finitely many generators. These assertions use the
definitions in [def-sbc-smooth-morphisms], [def-sbc-locally-finite-presentation],
[def-weil-closed-immersion], and [def-weil-finite-type-morphism]. Conversely, for any locally
finite type $f$, choose affine neighbourhoods $U=\operatorname{Spec}B$, $V=\operatorname{Spec}A$
with $f(U)\subseteq V$ and $B$ generated by $n$ elements over $A$. Sending the polynomial
variables to these generators gives a surjection $A[t_1,\ldots,t_n]\twoheadrightarrow B$, hence
a closed immersion $j:U\hookrightarrow\mathbf A^n_V$. Its ambient map is smooth by
[sbc-lem-affine-space-smooth]. More generally, if $P(f)$ and $j:U\to Q$ is any immersion over
$Y$ into a smooth $Y$-scheme, compare $j$ at each point with a defining witness for $P$ using
[lem-sfb-koszul-immersion-smooth-ambient-independence]. It follows that $j$ is Koszul-regular.
In particular this applies to the polynomial embeddings just constructed, after composing their
ambient smooth maps with $V\hookrightarrow Y$ as in step 1.

3. Let $b:V\to Y$ be etale and assume $P(f)$. Pull back each local factorization
$U\hookrightarrow Q\to Y$ to $V$. The pulled-back smooth morphism is smooth by
[lem-pc-proper-base-change](2). The map $Q\times_YV\to Q$ is etale by
[lem-weil-pbc-etale-scheme-base-change], hence flat by [def-weil-etale-morphism]. Therefore the
pulled-back closed immersion is Koszul-regular by
[lem-sfb-koszul-immersion-flat-ascent-etale-descent](1). The inverse images of the witness
source opens cover $X\times_YV$, and step 1 converts their local Koszul-regular immersions into
the required closed-immersion witnesses. This proves (ii).

4. Let $a:T\to X$ be etale and assume $P(f)$. Fix $t\in T$. Shrink a witness $U\hookrightarrow
Q\to Y$ around $a(t)$ so that $Q$ is affine; its inverse image $U$ is affine since it is closed
in $Q$. Choose an affine open $T_0\subseteq a^{-1}(U)$ containing $t$; the induced map $T_0\to
U$ is etale by [lem-weil-pbc-etale-composition-cancellation](1),(2),(3), since
$T_0\hookrightarrow T\xrightarrow{a}X$ is etale and $U\hookrightarrow X$ is an open immersion.
Apply [lem-weil-pbc-affine-etale-closed-immersion-lift] to $U\hookrightarrow Q$ and $T_0\to U$:
it gives an affine etale $W\to Q$ and a cartesian square $T_0=U\times_QW$. The immersion
$T_0\hookrightarrow W$ is Koszul-regular by
[lem-sfb-koszul-immersion-flat-ascent-etale-descent](1), since an etale map is flat. Also $W\to
Y$ is smooth: $W\to Q$ is smooth by [lem-weil-pbc-etale-smooth-zero-dimensional-fibres], and use
[lem-trf-smooth-composition]. These are witnesses for $P(fa)$ at every $t$, proving (i).

5. If $h:T\to V$ has $P$ and $b:V\to Y$ is etale, the same closed-immersion witnesses prove
$P(bh)$: their ambient smooth maps to $V$, composed with the smooth map $b$, are smooth by
[lem-weil-pbc-etale-smooth-zero-dimensional-fibres] and [lem-trf-smooth-composition]. If $P(f)$,
take $T=X$, $V=Y$ and both chart maps identities to obtain the witnesses in (iii) at every $x$.
It remains to prove the reverse implication of (iii).

6. Assume the pointwise chart condition of (iii). Step 5 gives $P(fa_x)$ for each chosen chart
$a_x:T_x\to X$, and the points $u_x$ show that these etale maps jointly cover $X$. By step 2
each $fa_x$ is locally of finite type; source locality for this property in
[lem-sfb-scheme-etale-source-target-properties] gives that $f$ is locally of finite type. Fix
affine $V\subseteq Y$ and affine $U\subseteq f^{-1}V$ as in step 2, with polynomial closed
immersion $j:U\hookrightarrow Q=\mathbf A^n_V$. By [lem-weil-pbc-etale-covering-base-change],
the charts $T_x\times_XU\to U$ form a jointly surjective etale family. Refine their sources by
affine opens $T_\alpha$; each $T_\alpha\to U$ is etale by
[lem-weil-pbc-etale-composition-cancellation](1),(2), and they are still jointly surjective.
Step 1 restricts $P(fa_x)$ to these opens, and restricts its target to $V$, giving
$P(T_\alpha\to V)$. No claim that $f$ is flat is used.

7. Lift each affine etale $T_\alpha\to U$ across $j$ using
[lem-weil-pbc-affine-etale-closed-immersion-lift], giving etale $q_\alpha:W_\alpha\to Q$ and
$T_\alpha=U\times_QW_\alpha$. The map $W_\alpha\to V$ is smooth by
[sbc-lem-affine-space-smooth], [lem-weil-pbc-etale-smooth-zero-dimensional-fibres], and
[lem-trf-smooth-composition]. Since $P(T_\alpha\to V)$, step 2 shows that its closed immersion
into $W_\alpha$ is Koszul-regular. Let $O=\bigcup_\alpha q_\alpha(W_\alpha)\subseteq Q$. It is
open by [lem-weil-pbc-etale-morphism-open-map], and it contains $j(U)$ because the $T_\alpha$
cover $U$. Thus $U\hookrightarrow O$ is a closed immersion; the maps $W_\alpha\to O$ are etale
by [lem-weil-pbc-etale-scheme-base-change], since $W_\alpha=W_\alpha\times_QO$, and jointly
surjective, and their pullbacks of this immersion are exactly $T_\alpha\hookrightarrow
W_\alpha$. Apply [lem-sfb-koszul-immersion-flat-ascent-etale-descent](2) to conclude that
$U\hookrightarrow O$ is Koszul-regular. As $O\to V$ is smooth by
[lem-pc-open-immersion-smooth-composition], this gives $P(f|_U)$, and composing to $Y$ uses step
1. Such $U$ cover $X$, so $P(f)$ holds. For empty $X$ the condition and conclusion are both
vacuous. Together with steps 1, 3, 4 and 5 this proves all assertions, and hence the property of
[def-sfb-etale-local-source-target-property].
-/
theorem scheme_lci_etale_locality :
    MorphismProperty.RespectsIso
      (fun (_ _ : Scheme.{u}) f ↦ RelativeFiniteHilbert.SchemeLci f) ∧
    IsEtaleLocalOnSourceAndTarget
      (fun (_ _ : Scheme.{u}) f ↦ RelativeFiniteHilbert.SchemeLci f) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
